{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "esnext",
    "lib": [
      "esnext"
    ],
    /* NEITHER `dom` NOR `node`: nothing here touches a document or a file. The research
       repository needs both - it renders to a canvas and reads the prover's output off
       disk - and this package is what is left when that is not in it, which is a theory
       that runs the same in a browser, on a server and in a worker. */
    "types": [],
    "allowJs": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "allowSyntheticDefaultImports": true,
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "strict": true,
    "strictNullChecks": false,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "module": "nodenext",
    "moduleResolution": "nodenext",
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "suppressImplicitAnyIndexErrors": false,
    "noEmit": true,
    "emitDecoratorMetadata": true,
    "experimentalDecorators": true
  },
  "include": [
    "src",
    "index.ts"
  ],
  /* THE VISUALS ARE NOT PART OF THAT. They draw on a canvas and write films to disk, so
     they need `dom` and `node` and the core deliberately has neither - see the note above.
     They are checked by `tsconfig.visuals.json` instead, which is the same settings with
     those two put back, so nothing about the theory is loosened to accommodate a picture. */
  "exclude": [
    "src/visuals",
    /* AND THE NOTATION IS NOT PART OF IT EITHER, for the same reason and one more.
       `src/notation` sets a proof into elements, so its panel reaches for `document` and
       it needs `dom` - which is the same exemption the visuals have. The one more is
       that it is the file that must never name a view library: it takes the runtime as
       an argument so that this package can depend on nothing and still be written in
       JSX at the far end. `tsconfig.notation.json` checks it, and checks it against
       `dom` alone rather than `node`, because unlike the visuals it never writes a
       file. */
    "src/notation",
    "src/lib/Report.ts",
    /* AND THE TWO FILES THAT READ A FILE. `Measured.ts` opens the column files this
       repository writes, and `Sparc.ts` is a borrowed catalogue read out of them - neither is
       theory, both need a filesystem, and the core is checked without one on purpose. They
       are covered by `tsconfig.visuals.json`, which checks everything. */
    "src/lib/Measured.ts",
    "src/lib/Sparc.ts"
  ]
}